  "notes": "UK TV-pop, disco- and Motown-informed: 84 and 117–130 straight with the backbeat on 2&4, and the research is explicit that the 167 catalogue reading on the debut is a doubled algorithmic value whose real spine is about 84 — that fold is why the session floor sits at 84 rather than in the 160s. Three drum facts are authored: \"4-on-the-floor on the club-leaning songs, otherwise kick 1/3\" (density 2–4 with the four-on-floor chorus flag left inherited and syncopation low), \"16th hat plus tambourine\", and \"hand-clap layers on every backbeat\", which is the tightened clap offset and the snap/tambourine perc pair. The bass is the entry's clearest fingerprint — \"octave-jumping disco bassline on the uptempos (8ths with a root–octave alternation)\" — so it runs `independent_riff` with the octave as its passing tone and a root-alternating cell. Harmony is the stated I–IV–V and I–vi–ii–V Motown loops plus the i–iv–VII minor vamp of the disco-facing singles. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so: tempo anchors and the producer connection are sourced, several parameters are convention-derived. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities, `bpm.mode` 120 and the melody density, which reads \"melodic range narrow, very high syllable density\".",
